<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Alids</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Alids are the descendants of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ali-ibn-abi-talib">Ali ibn Abi Talib</a>, cousin and son-in-law of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prophet-muhammad">Prophet Muhammad</a>, especially through his sons Hasan and Husayn. Many Muslims, above all the Shia, regarded them as the rightful leaders of the community, and Alid claimants led revolts against the Umayyad and Abbasid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliphs</a>. Alid dynasties ruled states such as the Idrisids in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a>, the Zaydis of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tabaristan">Tabaristan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yemen">Yemen</a> and the Ukhaydirids in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/alids.jpg" alt="Alids" width="320" height="480" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: José Luiz · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Arabic_caligraphic_seal_in_Hagia_Sophia.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Alids are the descendants of Ali, cousin and son-in-law of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prophet-muhammad">Prophet Muhammad</a>, the fourth <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rashidun-caliphate">Rashidun</a> caliph of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunni-islam">Sunni</a> tradition and the first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imam">Imam</a> in Shia Islam. Their two principal branches are the Hasanids and the Husaynids, descended from Hasan and Husayn, the sons of Ali and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fatima">Fatima</a>, the daughter of Muhammad. Sources report that Ali had seventeen daughters and eleven, fourteen or eighteen sons. The lineage also continued through Muhammad ibn al-Hanafiya, Abbas ibn Ali and Umar al-Atraf, whose descendants bore the title Alawi. The direct line beginning with Ali forms the Twelve Imams of Twelver Shia Islam, and as the family of Muhammad the Alids are revered by all Muslims.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>After Ali was assassinated in 661 and Mu'awiya founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umayyad-caliphate">Umayyad Caliphate</a>, the Alids and their supporters were heavily persecuted. Husayn and a small caravan were massacred by the Umayyads at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-karbala">Karbala</a> in 680, and al-Mukhtar rose in 685 on behalf of Muhammad ibn al-Hanafiya. Zayd ibn Ali led a failed uprising around 740, and his followers became the Zaydites. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasids</a> had rallied Shia support in the name of the family of Muhammad, but many were disillusioned when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abu-al-abbas-al-saffah">al-Saffah</a> declared himself <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a>, and the Abbasids soon turned on their former allies. Many Alids revolted, among them the Hasanid brothers Muhammad ibn Abd-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allah">Allah</a> and Ibrahim.</p><p>Some Alids took refuge in remote regions and founded dynasties. After the revolt of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/husayn-ibn-ali">Husayn ibn Ali</a> al-Abid was suppressed in 786, his brother Idris escaped and founded the first Alid dynasty in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a>; Zaydite rulers appeared in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a> and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yemen">Yemen</a>. Several <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imam">Imams</a> died under Abbasid surveillance or in prison, and Musa al-Kazim, the seventh, died in prison in 799. In 817 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">Caliph</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-mamun">al-Ma'mun</a> named <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ali-al-rida">Ali al-Rida</a> heir apparent, but the policy was reversed under opposition. Twelver Shia hold that the twelfth Imam has been in occultation since 874. After the death of Ja'far al-Sadiq in 765 the Isma'ilites separated from the Imamites, and their efforts culminated in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fatimid-caliphate">Fatimid Caliphate</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Several dynasties have claimed descent from Ali, often through Hasan. The Hasanid dynasties include the Idrisites and the Sharifs of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maghreb">Maghreb</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hammudid-dynasty">Hammudids</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andalusia">Andalusia</a>, while the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fatimid-caliphate">Fatimid Caliphate</a> claimed Husaynid descent, a claim some have questioned. The Alids remain central to the doctrine of the Shia, who limited leadership of the community to them after the Abbasid betrayal.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Alids” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Hashimites included the Abbasids themselves, descended from the Prophet’s uncle al-Abbas, and the <b>Alids</b>, descendants of his cousin and son-in-law Ali, whom the Shia held to be the rightful leaders of the community.</blockquote><a href="/societies/abbasid-caliphate">Social Hierarchy of the Abbasid Caliphate</a></li><li><blockquote>His authority depended on governors and tribal allies, and he faced revolts from the Kharijites of the Najdiyya and from al-Mukhtar's pro-<b>Alid</b> movement at Kufa, which Mus'ab defeated in 687.</blockquote><a href="/state/caliphate-of-ibn-al-zubayr">Caliphate of Ibn al-Zubayr</a></li><li><blockquote>They first appear in 791, when King Marzuban ibn Justan gave refuge to the <b>Alid</b> rebel Yahya ibn Abdallah, before handing him over to the Abbasid Caliphate.</blockquote><a href="/state/justanid-dynasty">Justanid dynasty</a></li><li><blockquote>Their land lay on pilgrimage and trade routes between Iraq, Bahrain and the Hejaz, and the Persian traveller Nasir Khusraw, who passed in the 1050s, mentions the fortified seat of its <b>Alid</b> emirs.</blockquote><a href="/state/ukhaydirid-emirate">Ukhaydirid Emirate</a></li></ul></section>
